# Modal Input Blocking — `ModalInputBlocker`

Table world input — hand cards, seat/table emoji targets — is driven by **camera
physics raycasts** gated by `TableInputGate` (shared core, `MonkeyTilt.Shared`),
not by uGUI raycasts. A full-screen backdrop Image blocks clicks on UI but does
**nothing** to world input: a modal that never pushes the gate lets players
hover/send emojis and click cards straight through it. This was a recurring
class of bug — every new modal had to remember paired push/pop calls.

## The pattern: one component, zero code

Author `ModalInputBlocker` (shared core, next to `TableInputGate`) on the
**root of every modal/overlay prefab that shows and hides via `SetActive`**.
It calls `TableInputGate.PushModal()` in `OnEnable` and `PopModal()` in
`OnDisable`, so world input is blocked for exactly the object's active
lifetime — nothing to call in `Show`/`Hide`, no held-gate flag to keep in
sync, no leaked gate when the object is destroyed while open.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — manual paired calls; forgetting either side is the recurring bug
public void Show() { TableInputGate.PushModal(); gameObject.SetActive(true); }

// ✅ GOOD — view only toggles visibility; the prefab root carries ModalInputBlocker
public void Show() { gameObject.SetActive(true); }
```

## When manual `PushModal` / `PopModal` is still right

- Gate hold **not tied to visibility** (e.g. a tutorial overlay that blocks
  input per coaching beat while staying visible).
- Code-built dev overlays with no prefab.
- Keep the guarded held-flag pattern there, and pop in `OnDestroy` too.

## Gotchas

- **Self-deactivating `Awake`**: if a view's `Awake` calls
  `gameObject.SetActive(false)` (start-hidden pattern), `OnEnable` never fires
  on that first activation, so no gate push. Such objects must start **active**
  in the scene and self-hide at load.
- Stacking is safe — the gate is depth-counted; two open modals = depth 2.
- Pointer-over blocking for non-modal UI (e.g. draggable chat) is a different
  mechanism: `WorldInputUiBlocker`, which blocks only while the pointer is over
  the marked subtree. Don't use it to gate modals, and vice versa.
- Debugging: `TableInputGate.GetEmojiGateStatus().Describe()` /
  `GetCardsGateStatus().Describe()` say exactly why input is blocked.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] New modal/overlay prefab root carries `ModalInputBlocker`
- [ ] No new manual `PushModal`/`PopModal` in SetActive-based views
- [ ] Verified in play mode: gate blocked while shown, released after hide
